# Copy to .env and edit. Never commit .env — it is gitignored.
#
# Quote any value containing a space. setup.sh and provision.sh `source` this
# file, so an unquoted space is parsed as a command and the script dies.
#
# There is one machine. Every value below is for the Debian target.

# ─── Identity ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Must match the `media` user created by setup.sh, which owns the media tree.
PUID=1000
PGID=1000
TZ=Africa/Johannesburg

# ─── Paths (host side only) ──────────────────────────────────────────────────
# DATA_ROOT is the host path backing /data. The container side is ALWAYS /data
# in every service — see spec §4.1. Never parameterise the container side.
DATA_ROOT=/data
CONFIG_ROOT=/opt/mediaserver

# ─── Hardware transcoding ────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# On the target:  getent group render
# Required — the stack refuses to start without it, so a missing value fails
# loudly rather than silently dropping Jellyfin to software transcoding.
RENDER_GID=

# ─── Network ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Address the service ports publish on.
#
# 0.0.0.0 is the intended end state: spec §5.1 wants direct LAN access, and it
# is also how a WireGuard peer reaches the admin apps. These ports are never
# proxied and never public — see docs/decisions.md D25.
#
# Start at 127.0.0.1 anyway, and widen once Jellyfin's and Jellyseerr's first-run
# wizards are done (README step 7). Those two are the only apps in the stack
# whose credentials cannot be pinned before first start, so until the wizards are
# finished an unclaimed admin account is being offered to anyone who can reach
# the port — including everyone else on the LAN (review-2026-08 S2).
BIND_ADDR=127.0.0.1

# Home LAN subnet, e.g. 192.168.1.0/24. REQUIRED, and required BEFORE the first
# setup.sh run — it decides whether the firewall is written at all. Three things
# break silently without it:
#   - setup.sh skips the entire DOCKER-USER block, so nothing filters the
#     published container ports while `ufw status` still reports default-deny
#   - the LAN cannot reach the service ports, so Infuse loses Jellyfin
#   - wg peers get no route to the LAN, so admin addresses stop resolving
#     off-network (it is half of INIT_ALLOWED_IPS)
LAN_SUBNET=

# ─── Public front door ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Only three names are served: the bare domain, jellyfin. and seerr. The seven
# admin apps are deliberately not routed — see caddy/sites.caddy.
#
# The LAN address the router forwards 80 and 443 to. Never a wildcard:
# validate.sh fails the build if Caddy publishes on 0.0.0.0.
CADDY_BIND_ADDR=
# Fully qualified, with A records for it plus jellyfin.<domain> and
# seerr.<domain> pointing at the WAN address.
PUBLIC_DOMAIN=CHANGEME.example.com
# Let's Encrypt contact address, used for expiry warnings.
ACME_EMAIL=

# ACME directory. Leave commented for production.
#
# Uncomment to prove out DNS and the port forwards against staging first.
# Production allows five failed validations per hostname per hour, and the two
# mistakes that burn them — port 80 not forwarded, a record that has not
# propagated — are only discoverable by trying. Staging issues from an untrusted
# root, so a browser warning is the expected result and means everything else
# worked. Restart Caddy after changing this, and comment it out again before the
# household is pointed at the domain.
#CADDY_ACME_CA=https://acme-staging-v02.api.letsencrypt.org/directory

# Where the landing page's Manage links point: this box's LAN address. Those
# apps are not proxied, so the links are <lan-ip>:<port> and only render for
# clients on the LAN or the tunnel. One value, and it has to work from both —
# which is why WireGuard runs on the host and routes the LAN (decisions.md D26).
ADMIN_HOST=

# ─── Remote access (WireGuard, via wg-easy) ──────────────────────────────────
# How the administrator reaches the seven admin surfaces from outside the house.
# The household never needs this — they use the public domain above.
#
# The container runs with host networking, so wg0 is a real host interface and a
# peer reaches <lan-ip>:<port> by exactly the address it would use at home.
#
# What peers dial: a name with an A record, or the WAN address. A name is
# better, because a changed WAN address is then one DNS edit rather than
# reissuing every peer config.
WG_HOST=vpn.CHANGEME.example.com
# The tunnel port. Forward this UDP from the router.
WG_PORT=51820
# The admin UI. LAN and tunnel only — NEVER forward this one.
WG_UI_PORT=51821
# The address the UI binds to. Never a wildcard; this is the box's LAN address,
# the same value as ADMIN_HOST. validate.sh fails the build on 0.0.0.0.
WG_UI_BIND=

# First-start admin credentials for the wg-easy UI, in plain text — v15 removed
# PASSWORD_HASH and refuses to start if it sees it.
#
# These apply ONLY on the very first container start; after that the credentials
# live in wg-easy's database and changing them here does nothing. Change the
# password in the UI. Treat these as passwords: .env is 0600 for a reason.
#
# WG_PASS is generated by `./scripts/provision.sh --init-keys` along with the
# other passwords. It is a bootstrap credential rather than a rotatable one, for
# the reason above — but it gates minting a VPN peer, and a peer is on the LAN,
# so it should not be the one password left to a human to invent.
WG_USER=admin
WG_PASS=

# The tunnel's own subnets. v4 and v6 are one group in wg-easy and must both be
# set. 10.8.0.0/24 is inside 10.0.0.0/8, which caddy/sites.caddy already treats
# as private — pick something outside RFC1918 and the Manage section stops
# rendering for tunnel clients until you widen that snippet.
WG_SUBNET=10.8.0.0/24
WG_SUBNET6=fdcc:ad94:bacf:61a3::/64
# Resolver handed to peers.
WG_DNS=1.1.1.1

# ─── Host ports ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Container-side ports are fixed; only these host-side ones move.
JELLYFIN_PORT=8096
PROWLARR_PORT=9696
SONARR_PORT=8989
RADARR_PORT=7878
BAZARR_PORT=6767
QBIT_PORT=8080
QBIT_TORRENT_PORT=6881
SAB_PORT=8085
SEERR_PORT=5055

# Quality profiles Jellyseerr hands to Radarr and Sonarr for new requests.
# These names must match what Recyclarr creates — see config/recyclarr/recyclarr.yml.
# provision.sh looks them up by name rather than id, since ids depend on the
# order things were created in.
SEERR_RADARR_PROFILE="UHD-2160p"
SEERR_SONARR_PROFILE="WEB-1080p"
CADDY_HTTP_PORT=80
CADDY_HTTPS_PORT=443

# ─── API keys ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Pinned rather than read out of each app's UI. The *arrs accept their key as a
# runtime environment variable, so setting it here means the key is known before
# the container has ever started — which is what lets scripts/provision.sh wire
# the stack together, and what Recyclarr authenticates with.
#
# Generate three distinct 32-char hex values:
#   openssl rand -hex 16
#
# Or let the provisioner do it:  ./scripts/provision.sh --init-keys
#
# Treat these as passwords. A valid *arr API key is full control of that app.
SONARR_API_KEY=
RADARR_API_KEY=
PROWLARR_API_KEY=

# Sonarr / Radarr / Prowlarr web UI login, applied to all three.
#
# Without this a fresh box serves an open account-creation screen on every *arr
# port until a human gets to it, and the first visitor becomes the admin.
# provision.sh sets the credentials over the API before anyone logs in, which
# only works because the API keys above are pinned ahead of first start.
#
# The auth *method* is not here — it is pinned in docker-compose.yml as
# SONARR__AUTH__METHOD / __REQUIRED so it is re-applied on every container
# start. Overriding those two below is for debugging only; see the warning
# there before changing them.
#
# To rotate: change ARR_PASS and re-run with ARR_AUTH_FORCE=1 ./scripts/provision.sh
ARR_USER=admin
ARR_PASS=

# qBittorrent Web UI credentials. qBittorrent generates a random temporary
# password on every start until a permanent one is set; provision.sh sets this
# one, after which the temporary password stops being issued.
QBIT_USER=admin
QBIT_PASS=

# SABnzbd and Bazarr ship with NO web UI login at all. Both are worth closing:
# SABnzbd runs post-processing scripts (arbitrary execution, same class of risk
# as qBittorrent's external-program setting), and Bazarr has full write access
# to the library. provision.sh sets these; leaving them blank leaves the UIs
# open to anyone who can reach the port.
#
# Setting these does not affect Sonarr/Radarr, which authenticate to SABnzbd
# with its API key rather than these credentials.
SAB_USER=admin
SAB_PASS=
BAZARR_USER=admin
BAZARR_PASS=

# ─── Usenet provider (news server) ───────────────────────────────────────────
# The provider stores the actual articles; the indexers below only say where
# they are. Both are required — indexers alone download nothing.
#
# Defaults are Eweka's published settings. provision.sh warns loudly rather
# than failing silently if USENET_USER is blank.
USENET_HOST=news.eweka.nl
USENET_PORT=563
USENET_SSL=true
USENET_CONNECTIONS=20
USENET_USER=
USENET_PASS=

# ─── Usenet indexers ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# API keys from each site's profile page. Blank keys are skipped, so you can
# add one now and the other later. Both are Newznab; Prowlarr has built-in
# definitions, so only the key is needed.
NZBGEEK_API_KEY=
NZBPLANET_API_KEY=

# ─── Torrent indexers ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Public, no account required, so provision.sh enables these by default.
#
# Two halves. Nyaa.si, SubsPlease and AnimeTosho carry the anime side — Usenet
# covers anime poorly, so without them Sonarr finds almost nothing for an anime
# series. The Pirate Bay and YTS carry everything else: without them the torrent
# side is anime-only and all TV and film depends on the Usenet provider and
# indexer subscriptions both staying live. They are the fallback for a lapsed
# subscription and for titles that have aged out of Usenet retention.
#
# Comma-separated Prowlarr definition names. Note "AnimeTosho" and not
# "Anime Tosho": Prowlarr ships both, and despite its private label the former
# needs no credentials and works, while the semiprivate one fails to connect.
#
# 1337x is the best general-purpose public tracker and is deliberately not here:
# it is behind CloudFlare and Prowlarr rejects it on save. To use it, add a
# FlareSolverr container, add it under Prowlarr > Settings > Indexer Proxies,
# tag 1337x with that proxy, then append 1337x to this list. That is an extra
# always-on headless Chrome, which is why it is opt-in.
#
# Prowlarr validates each indexer when it is saved, so a definition that cannot
# be reached fails loudly here rather than silently returning no results later.
#
# Add private trackers through the Prowlarr UI — they need per-site credentials
# and are not worth templating here.
# Quote it — the value contains spaces, and setup.sh/provision.sh source this
# file, where an unquoted space would be parsed as a command.
TORRENT_INDEXERS="Nyaa.si,SubsPlease,AnimeTosho,The Pirate Bay,YTS"

# ─── Download client hostnames ───────────────────────────────────────────────
# How the *arrs reach the download clients across the compose bridge. Leave
# these alone until the VPN lands (spec §7): qBittorrent then moves to
# `network_mode: service:gluetun` and stops having a network identity of its
# own, so the *arrs have to reach it at `gluetun` instead. Set QBIT_HOST=gluetun
# that day and re-run provision.sh.
# QBIT_HOST=qbittorrent
# SAB_HOST=sabnzbd

# ─── smartd alerting (consumed by setup.sh, not by compose) ──────────────────
# msmtp relays SMART alerts through this SMTP server. Without a working relay
# smartd alerts fail silently, which defeats the point of monitoring a
# single-drive array — see docs/decisions.md Q3.
ALERT_EMAIL=
SMTP_HOST=
SMTP_PORT=587
SMTP_USER=
SMTP_PASS=
SMTP_FROM=
